7.3 Rules for Type 1: Highlighted bounding box

Action

Rule

Add

Reviewer draws a box on the exact part of the answer and picks a colour. A feedback comment is mandatory before the box can be saved.

Move / resize

Box stays within the page it is on. Feedback comment stays attached.

Change colour

Allowed between red, yellow and green. Polarity follows the colour: red is negative, yellow is neutral, green is positive. The comment keeps the same number and colour as its box.

Edit feedback

Free text. Cannot be empty. Reviewer is guided to keep one point per box. A comment with both praise and a mistake should be split into two boxes (Phase 1 §4.4).

Delete

Box and its comment are removed together. Remaining boxes renumber.

Numbering

Numbers are auto-assigned by position (top to bottom, page by page) and update automatically on add, move or delete. Reviewer never types a number. No number is repeated within the answer sheet (Phase 1 User Story 2).

Tone

Same meaning for all cohorts. Foundation feedback is simple and encouraging, UPSC is analytical and exam-oriented.

No feedback case

If a point needs no comment, the reviewer uses a Type 2 mark instead of a box.

7.4 Rules for Type 2: Handwritten teacher-style marks

Mark

Add

Edit / change

Delete

Rules

Tick / Cross

Click on the answer

Switch between tick and cross; move

Yes

No feedback box.

Underline

Drag on the word or line

Colour red (mistake) or green (good point); resize; move; add, edit or remove the optional comment

Yes

The only Type 2 mark that can carry a comment. If a comment exists it shows with the underline, otherwise the underline shows alone.

Strike-through

Drag over the content

Resize; move

Yes

For wrong or irrelevant content.

Circled score

Click next to the question or part

Edit value; move

Yes

Shown in red, handwritten-style as in Phase 1. Set by the reviewer through the toolkit.

Text stamp

Pick from the list, click to place

Replace with another stamp; move

Yes

Maximum one stamp per question. No stamp on an unattempted question (it shows 0 marks). No stamp where the answer only needs a tick or cross (objective type).

All Type 2 marks stay on the exact part of the answer they refer to, and stay in place while scrolling and across multi-page answers. They never open a feedback box (except the optional underline comment).

7.5 Validation on Submit Review

Check

Result if it fails

Every Type 1 box has a non-empty feedback comment

Submit blocked. The box is highlighted in the workspace.

Every question's marks are present and between 0 and its max marks

Submit blocked. The question is flagged in the navigator.

UPSC: overall summary filled where the cohort shows it

Submit blocked.

Totals and remark recomputed from the latest marks

Automatic. Not a reviewer action.

8. Epics & User Stories

Numbering: UST = student, USA = admin / Batch Manager, USS = SEP reviewer. All student-side behaviour applies to Web, Android and iOS unless stated. For screen-level specifics, refer to the designs "as given in figma".

Epic 1: Attempt a multi-question subjective test online

As a student, I should be able to attempt a test with multiple questions, uploading my complete answer copy once and submitting the test once, so that I get one complete evaluation.

UST101: Question list for a multi-question test

Acceptance Criteria:

From All Tests → Subjective tab, tapping a multi-question test opens one screen with all its questions in a single scrolling list, in order, as given in figma.

The top of the screen shows the countdown timer for the test duration, the test name, and the test summary: number of questions, total marks and duration (for example 10 Questions | 250 Marks | 180 min).

Each question in the list shows its question number (Ques 1, Ques 2, ...), its marks and the full question text. Questions are separated by a divider.

An Upload Answers button stays fixed at the bottom of the screen while the student scrolls through the questions.

The student can read all the questions and write the answers on their own paper before uploading.

Single-question tests continue to open exactly as today.

Edge cases: question text fails to load (show retry, do not allow submit); student force-closes the app mid-test (uploaded answers and statuses are restored on reopen); test opened on a second device (statuses reflect the latest uploads); old app version that does not support multi-question tests (handled as in UST501).

UST102: Upload the answer copy once

Acceptance Criteria:

Tapping Upload Answers opens the upload step, as given in figma.

The student uploads the complete answer copy for the whole test in one go, either as a PDF or as images (more than one image is allowed), using the existing upload method. The student does not upload question by question.

Before submitting the test, the student can replace the upload.

After a successful upload, the quality check runs (see UST103).

Edge cases: upload fails because of file size (clear message with the allowed limit); no network (retry, upload is not marked successful); network drops mid-upload; double-tap on upload; several images where one fails to upload (the upload is not accepted until all images are uploaded); app killed during upload.

UST103: Copy quality check with one-time re-upload

Acceptance Criteria:

After the upload, the copy is checked for: (1) it is readable and evaluation is possible (not blurry), (2) it is handwritten, (3) it has content that can be evaluated.

The check runs on the whole copy and finds which question has an issue and which check failed.

If all checks pass, the copy is accepted.

If a check fails, the student sees which question and which check failed, and is asked to re-upload the answer for that question, one time only. Questions that passed are not asked again.

If the re-upload also fails a check, or the student has already re-uploaded once for that question, the answer is accepted as it is and the student can submit the test. The student is not blocked, and nothing is flagged to the reviewer.

Edge cases: check service is slow or fails (copy is accepted without a check result; student is not blocked); student replaces the upload later (the check runs again, and the one-time re-upload rule is counted per question across all replacements); more than one question fails at the same time (all are shown, and each can be re-uploaded once); multi-page PDF where only some pages fail.

UST104: Submit the full test

Acceptance Criteria:

The student submits the test once, after uploading the answer copy. Submit is disabled until a copy is uploaded.

If the check found questions that have no answer in the copy, a confirmation lists them before submit. Unattempted questions get 0 marks (Phase 1 §4.3 C).

After submit, the upload cannot be changed and the student sees the evaluation-in-progress state, as given in figma.

Edge cases: submit with no network (not marked submitted, retry available); double-tap on submit (one submission only); app closed right after submit (status is restored from the server on reopen); test timer ends while the student is mid-upload (follows existing test rules, unchanged).

Epic 2: Offline multi-question tests

As a Batch Manager, I should be able to upload the physical copies of a multi-question test through admin, so that offline students get the same evaluation and result as online students.

USA201: BM uploads the copy for a multi-question test

Acceptance Criteria:

The BM uploads one complete scanned copy per student for the test, using the existing admin entry point. The upload flow stays the same as today.

Multi-question tests are available for selection in the same way as single-question tests.

After upload, the copy goes to AI evaluation and then to SEP, the same as the online flow.

No quality check is applied to the BM's upload, same as today.

Edge cases: upload fails (clear error, BM can retry); wrong student or wrong test mapped (existing admin correction behaviour, unchanged); a copy uploaded twice for the same student and test (latest one is used; confirmation shown); pages missing or out of order in the scan.

UST202: Offline student sees the New Result Experience

Acceptance Criteria:

Once the reviewer submits the review, an offline student sees the result in the New Result Experience on Web, Android and iOS, the same as an online student.

The student's own answer copy and Download → My Answer Paper use the BM-uploaded scan.

Edge cases: student has no app login or account mapping (existing behaviour, unchanged); result opened on an old app version (UST501).

Epic 3: Evaluation, review and result release

As a student, I should receive one reviewed result for my whole test, so that I see the final marks and feedback only after a human has checked them.

UST301: Test-wise evaluation and reviewed result

Acceptance Criteria:

AI evaluates the full test (all questions) once the test is submitted (online) or the copy is uploaded (offline).

The evaluated copy is sent to SEP. The student does not see the AI draft at any point.

When the reviewer submits the review, the final reviewed result is released and opens in the Phase 1 New Result Experience: cover page with question-wise marks table, annotated answer copy, Model Answer tab, Hide/Show Feedback, Report, Download (3 files) and Rating, all unchanged from Phase 1.

The cover page, annotations, marks and feedback shown to the student match exactly what the reviewer submitted.

Until the review is submitted the student continues to see the evaluation-in-progress state.

Edge cases: AI evaluation fails for a test (copy is not lost, goes to a retry / manual path, existing behaviour); reviewer submits while the student has the result screen open (updated on next open or refresh).

Epic 4: SEP reviewer toolkit

As a reviewer, I should be able to edit, remove and add every element of the student's result using a toolkit, so that the final result reflects my judgement and not only the AI draft.

USS401: Review workspace for a multi-question copy

Acceptance Criteria:

The reviewer opens a copy in a workspace that shows the student's answer copy exactly as the student will see it (Phase 1 annotated view), with the toolkit in 8.2.

A question navigator lists all questions with marks and attempted / unattempted state.

The reviewer can move across questions and pages without losing unsaved changes. Annotations stay anchored to their page and position at every zoom level.

AI-generated annotations load as editable items. Items changed or added by the reviewer are marked as edited.

Work is auto-saved as a draft. Closing and reopening the copy restores the draft.

Edge cases: browser refresh or session timeout (draft restored); very long or many-page answers (workspace stays responsive); copy with no AI annotations (reviewer can add from scratch).

USS402: Edit highlighted bounding boxes (Type 1)

Acceptance Criteria:

The reviewer can add, move, resize and delete boxes, change a box's colour between red / yellow / green, and edit its feedback, following the rules in 8.3.

A box cannot be saved without a feedback comment.

Box numbers and comment numbers are assigned automatically in position order and always match, with the same colour.

Changing a colour changes the polarity of that feedback.

Every change appears in the student result exactly as designed in Phase 1 (numbered box with matching comment, opening in position order).

Edge cases: box drawn across two pages (not allowed, box stays on one page); box drawn outside the answer area; overlapping boxes (allowed, each remains selectable); deleting a box that a student has already reacted to with thumbs (the reaction data is retained for DS, see Tracking).

USS403: Edit handwritten-style marks (Type 2)

Acceptance Criteria:

The reviewer can add, change, move, resize (where applicable) and delete ticks, crosses, underlines, strike-throughs, circled scores and stamps, following the rules in 8.4.

An underline can optionally carry a comment, which the reviewer can add, edit or remove. No other Type 2 mark carries a comment.

Only one stamp per question is allowed, and none on an unattempted question.

The marks look and behave in the student result exactly as in Phase 1 (placed on the exact part of the answer, no feedback box, stay in place while scrolling and across pages).

Edge cases: stamp added where a tick / cross already marks an objective answer (blocked); mark placed on a blank area of the page; very small underline or strike-through (minimum size enforced so it can be selected).

USS404: Edit marks, cover page and totals

Acceptance Criteria:

The reviewer can edit marks obtained per question in the marks panel, within 0 to the question's max marks, decimals allowed.

Total marks obtained, total marks and the remark update automatically on every change using the Phase 1 rules (sum of questions; remark from marks %, per cohort configuration). The reviewer cannot edit them directly.

The cover page the student sees shows the same figures, and the question-wise table has one row per question, with 0 for unattempted questions.

Student details on the cover page are read-only.

Edge cases: marks entered above max or below 0 (field error, not saved); non-numeric input; a question with no AI marks (reviewer must enter marks before submit); marks changed after thumbs / reports exist on that question (existing reports are kept).

